                      \\text{If}\;\sum_{i \ge 1} \theta_i = \pi,\;\text{then}\;f_2(\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_3,\dots) &amp;= \sum_{i \ge 1} \sin^2 \theta_i. \\
                      \\text{If}\;\sum_{i \ge 1} \theta_i = \pi,\;\text{then}\;f_3(\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_3,\dots) &amp;= \sum_{i \ge 1} \frac{\sin(2\theta_i)}{2}.
                      \\end{aligned}
          %p
            There is a simple geometric interpretation of the expression on the right side of the identity involving
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i, i \ge 1
            are the angles between adjacent diagonals of a polygon inscribed in a circle of unit diameter. For present purposes we consider the sides of the polygon to be “diagonals”. (All but two of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i
            appear at each vertex. For example, in an octagon, at one vertex the angles are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_1,\dots,\theta_6
            at the next vertex they are
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_2,\dots,\theta_7
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 8
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_3,\dots,\theta_8
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 8
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_4,\dots,\theta_8,\theta_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_3
            is half the area of the polygon. Each term on the right side of the identity is half the signed area of a triangle having one vertex at the center of the circle. As one goes counterclockwise around the polygon, the signed area is positive or negative as the ray from the center is then turning counterclockwise or clockwise. The terms on the left side may perhaps not admit so simple an interpretation when the number of nonzero
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i
            is more than 4. The special case involving only three nonzero
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i
            is the well-known (?) identity that says that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mi β
                  %mo +
                  %mi γ
                  %mo =
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha+\beta+\gamma=\pi
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi sin
                    %mi α
                    %mi sin
                    %mi β
                    %mi sin
                    %mi γ
                    %mo =
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4\sin\alpha\sin\beta\sin\gamma = sin(2\alpha) + \sin(2\beta) + \sin(2\gamma)
          %p
            (Is there any simple geometric interpretation of the identity involving
            = succeed "?)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
