          %h3#a_question A question
          %p Are these four identities the first four terms in a sequence that continues?
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hardy"}> Michael Hardy
              \: A student in India whom I encountered via the internet told me that the problem of proving the “conditional identity” that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi sin
                    %mi α
                    %mi sin
                    %mi β
                    %mi sin
                    %mi γ
                    %mo =
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4\sin\alpha\sin\beta\sin\gamma = sin(2\alpha) + \sin(2\beta) + \sin(2\gamma)
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo +
                    %mi β
                    %mo +
                    %mi γ
                    %mo =
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha+\beta+\gamma=\pi
              recurs perennially on the joint entrance exam of the Indian Institutes of Technology. Hence “well known”.
